@page "/learn/patient"
@model SmartOnFhirDemo.Pages.Learn.PatientModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "The patient read";
}

<partial name="_Progress" model="new[] { Model.Step.Number }" />

<partial name="_Step" model="Model.Step" />

<section class="step">
    <h2>The summary this app renders</h2>
    <p>
        The projection of that resource down to seven fields — the whole of what this
        particular app was built to do, and the reason it needed everything above.
    </p>
    <partial name="_Summary" model="Model.Summary" />
</section>

<section class="step">
    <h2>Reading on, now that the launch is still open</h2>
    <p>
        Everything above happened during the handshake. These happen now: press one and the
        app makes a fresh request with the same token, because step ⑥ kept it. Each is a
        <em>search</em> rather than a read by id — <code>Condition?patient={id}</code>, not
        <code>Condition/{id}</code> — which is the shape a <code>patient/Condition.read</code>
        scope actually authorises: a class of data about one patient, not one URL. Every one
        of them writes a row to the access log, exactly as the two reads above did.
    </p>
</section>

<partial name="_Chart" model="Model.Chart" />

<p class="next"><a href="/">&larr; Back to the start</a></p>

<p class="aside">
    The access token is still here, held against your browser as step ⑥ described, and it
    goes when the EHR said it would. Nothing on this page is written down: the account that
    made the walkthrough readable never held the credential, and both are in memory only.
    The one thing that does reach disk is the access log — a row per request the app made
    to the EHR, and none of them carrying what was read.
</p>
